    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. One more thing though—I've been hearing from the dev team that we still get these weird, unpredictable latency spikes or tiny errors that appear out of nowhere and then vanish. It's like something is just messing with the resource allocation or causing minor glitches in the background. How do we explain those kinds of \"ghost\" issues to the stakeholders in a way that doesn't make it sound like our infrastructure is just unstable? Or is there a way AWS Auto Scaling helps mitigate those random, small-scale disruptions?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those unpredictable latency spikes and vanishing errors are often symptomatic of resource contention or transient load imbalances, which is a common, albeit annoying, characteristic of highly distributed systems. Instead of framing it as \"instability,\" you can present it as the system momentarily struggling to perfectly balance the load across all available resources under fluctuating demand. A well-tuned Auto Scaling group mitigates this by providing rapid, automated response to these tiny dips or spikes in utilization. It ensures that if a service momentarily gets overwhelmed by a small surge of requests—a micro-burst of traffic—it can quickly spin up another instance to absorb the load before the user even notices a noticeable delay. This proactive scaling acts like a constant, invisible guard against those fleeting resource bottlenecks.",
      "source": "target",
